ity space  and  if you look at what programs they've  been doing right now i mean  you know seniors right efforts they told  us they're not interested in cooking  they won't cook  but look at the left you see all these  very interesting magics and you know  seniors started you know being part of  the process they started coming down for  the programs  they started being interested in the  space and i remember very clearly during  the launch of the project  one of the aunties actually came to me  and said like you know  i really want to thank you for you know  designing this kitchen because this is  not something that i thought would even  be possible  i never thought our center could look  like that and that to me was the  greatest takeaway because  by just listening to the community and  giving them a voice  it may be a very simple thing to do but  it really really means a lot to the  community  and the whole sense of home and  ownership will then be strengthened  and this is actually a very good example  of uh how we use participatory design  to give you know the community a voice  and ownership  and building that sense of home within  the community  and um of course in this last example  i'm going to share with you today  um it's a bigger scale township planning  program that we have done so in  singapore we have this thing called the  neighborhood renewal program  whereby um you know the government will  give you a sum of money and  you know in the past how the town  councils are doing is that you know they  would just mail out survey forms for  everybody  and you just fill up the survey form and  that's it you know uh  and they collaborate the survey form you  want to see this business and i just  delivered that to you right  but i think in this case um the  minister of the the member of parliament  in this area wanted to do something a  bit different  so um they were actually asking pid if  we can come into this neighborhood  to you know engage the residents o